The critical design principle

The Chama application is provider-agnostic.

It should be able to say:

await payment_service.collect(
    chama_id=chama.id,
    member_id=member.id,
    amount=1000
)

without knowing whether the underlying payment is:

Jenga
KCB BUNI
NCBA
Daraja

The PaymentProviderService decides.

That gives you a genuinely extensible Kenyan financial platform rather than a "Jenga Chama app."

And because KCB BUNI currently advertises interoperability across all Kenyan banks/payment networks, it is worth treating BUNI as an important provider candidate for broad payment-network coverage, while retaining bank-specific adapters for account data and reconciliation. 

For production, each bank/provider still needs its own onboarding, credentials, approved products, limits and commercial agreement. KCB, for example, explicitly requires a KCB or VOOMA account for production BUNI API use. 

This is the architecture I would lock in before writing the first payment integration.
